$DIR/main.p:26:14: b may have been moved into take2 (its parameter takes a Buf by value) at line 28, but the deferred call (line 26) uses it when the function returns
    Buf is an owner type: its values own what they hold and release it when they
    are dropped. b is moved into take2 (its parameter takes a Buf by value) at
    line 28 on some paths to this point and not on others. On those paths b
    holds nothing afterwards, and the compiler cannot let a value be used where
    it may already belong to someone else. The call deferred at line 26 keeps a
    view of b in its receiver or arguments and runs when the function returns,
    after line 28, so it would look at what b no longer holds.
    Go copies such values freely and its garbage collector frees what they refer
    to when nothing refers to it any more. Plutonium has no garbage collector:
    an owner frees what it owns when it is dropped, so there must be exactly
    one.
    Give b a new value after line 28, so that the deferred call finds one:
        b = Buf{}
    Or do the work of the deferred call at the end of the function, before line
    28, instead of deferring it
    (see "Moves" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.5)
    (run "pluto explain E3302" for a longer explanation)
